WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.016 --> 00:00:08.220
[MUSIC]

00:00:08.220 --> 00:00:11.916
One of the core findings of our research,
is that the political arguments

00:00:11.916 --> 00:00:15.493
that are going to are going to be
effective for building a bigger consensus

00:00:15.493 --> 00:00:18.905
in the US is usually an argument
you haven't heard before.

00:00:18.905 --> 00:00:23.076
It's an argument that advocates for
a policy or a candidate in terms of

00:00:23.076 --> 00:00:27.685
the values and beliefs of the people
that are being targeted for persuasion.

00:00:27.685 --> 00:00:32.910
[MUSIC]

00:00:32.910 --> 00:00:35.100
We've tested this idea both ways.

00:00:35.100 --> 00:00:39.210
We've looked at whether liberal policy
positions do better when they're

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:41.970
framed in terms of conservative values and
we find that they do

00:00:41.970 --> 00:00:45.380
because they pull greater support from
conservatives and sometimes moderates.

00:00:45.380 --> 00:00:48.920
And we've also looked at whether
conservative policy positions perform

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:52.240
better when they're reframed in
terms of liberal values, and

00:00:52.240 --> 00:00:53.600
again, we find that they do.

00:00:53.600 --> 00:00:58.322
So for example, if you were to reframe
military spending as consistent with

00:00:58.322 --> 00:01:03.193
equality of opportunity and social
justice, making the case for the military

00:01:03.193 --> 00:01:07.842
as an institution where the poor and
minorities can achieve on a relatively

00:01:07.842 --> 00:01:13.082
level playing field with other Americans,
liberals will be more open to supporting

00:01:13.082 --> 00:01:17.624
high levels of military spending after
they hear an argument like that.

00:01:17.624 --> 00:01:22.745
[MUSIC]

00:01:22.745 --> 00:01:26.544
A few years ago, we got really interested
in same sex marriage attitudes, and we're

00:01:26.544 --> 00:01:30.190
interested in whether certain persuasive
techniques might be more effective for

00:01:30.190 --> 00:01:32.450
building support for same sex marriage.

00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:37.660
And so to explore this idea,
we ran an experiment where we had people

00:01:37.660 --> 00:01:41.730
either read a relatively conventional
argument for same sex marriage that said

00:01:41.730 --> 00:01:46.060
that gay marriage should be legal because
of the values of equality, fairness,

00:01:46.060 --> 00:01:48.810
gay people should have the same
rights as anybody else.

00:01:48.810 --> 00:01:52.230
We compared the effects of that
message to a very different message

00:01:52.230 --> 00:01:56.210
that made the case for same sex marriage
in terms of the values of patriotism and

00:01:56.210 --> 00:01:57.330
group loyalty.

00:01:57.330 --> 00:02:01.730
And it said that, gay people are proud,
patriotic Americans,

00:02:01.730 --> 00:02:05.870
they serve in the military, they
contribute to our economy and our society.

00:02:05.870 --> 00:02:09.180
And for that reason, they deserve
the same rights as anybody else.

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:13.330
And what we found is that that second
message was significantly more persuasive

00:02:13.330 --> 00:02:17.250
and it was especially more persuasive
with conservatives who are normally

00:02:17.250 --> 00:02:19.270
reluctant to support same sex marriage,

00:02:19.270 --> 00:02:21.210
especially at the time we ran
the study a few years ago.

00:02:21.210 --> 00:02:24.801
But then when they heard that argument
in terms of their own values,

00:02:24.801 --> 00:02:28.203
more conservative values around
patriotism, group loyalty,

00:02:28.203 --> 00:02:31.290
they said okay,
like this is starting to speak to me,

00:02:31.290 --> 00:02:34.252
and some of them moved their
positions on these issues.

00:02:34.252 --> 00:02:39.603
[MUSIC]

00:02:39.603 --> 00:02:44.042
Most recently, we ran an experiment on
how to make the most persuasive appeal

00:02:44.042 --> 00:02:48.074
you could for an economically
progressive political candidate, and

00:02:48.074 --> 00:02:50.830
specifically a presidential candidate.

00:02:50.830 --> 00:02:54.610
So we're interested in is there a way
to build greater levels of support for

00:02:54.610 --> 00:02:56.030
economically progressive candidates.

00:02:56.030 --> 00:02:59.310
And the reason we were interested in
this is that if you look historically,

00:02:59.310 --> 00:03:03.460
Americans are actually quite
supportive of federal jobs programs,

00:03:03.460 --> 00:03:05.950
minimum wage increases, paid family leave,

00:03:05.950 --> 00:03:08.370
these sorts of programs,
they poll very well in America.

00:03:08.370 --> 00:03:11.560
But Americans don't necessarily vote for
the candidates that would pass those

00:03:11.560 --> 00:03:14.130
policies, and so
they don't get passed at a high rate.

00:03:14.130 --> 00:03:15.840
So we're very interested in this puzzle.

00:03:15.840 --> 00:03:18.700
So what we did was we
constructed an experiment

00:03:18.700 --> 00:03:23.230
where we had a candidate who supported
these pretty popular policies.

00:03:23.230 --> 00:03:27.135
And we either had them talk about those
policies in terms of classically liberal

00:03:27.135 --> 00:03:32.045
values like equality, social justice,
protecting vulnerable people, or we

00:03:32.045 --> 00:03:36.405
had them talk about them in what are often
thought to be more conservative values of

00:03:36.405 --> 00:03:40.485
respect for tradition, caring for family,
the American dream, things like that.

00:03:40.485 --> 00:03:44.945
And what we found was that candidate, that
second candidate that talked in terms of

00:03:44.945 --> 00:03:49.250
more conservative values, but
about economically progressive policies,

00:03:49.250 --> 00:03:54.290
was clearly the most popular candidate
in the experiment that we ran.

00:03:54.290 --> 00:03:58.178
They gained extra support from
moderates and conservatives alike.

00:03:58.178 --> 00:04:03.741
And they were essentially in the study
we ran, a shoo in to win an election.

00:04:03.741 --> 00:04:08.947
[MUSIC]

00:04:08.947 --> 00:04:12.826
One thing that I think is really
attractive about moral reframing is it

00:04:12.826 --> 00:04:16.966
offers an alternative to our only real
technique that we think of now as a way

00:04:16.966 --> 00:04:20.035
to build political consensus,
which is compromise.

00:04:20.035 --> 00:04:24.620
Compromise on policy,
move to the perceived middle, and

00:04:24.620 --> 00:04:28.920
that's going to be the way to pick up
51% of voters or get something passed.

00:04:28.920 --> 00:04:33.420
And that's an unsatisfying tool
if it's the only tool you have.

00:04:33.420 --> 00:04:36.220
And so it's attractive, I think,
to have another alternative.

00:04:36.220 --> 00:04:39.513
Another way that you could add voters,
add people to your

00:04:39.513 --> 00:04:44.041
base of support besides just changing
the policy positions you advocate for,

00:04:44.041 --> 00:04:47.695
especially if you think those
are the right policy positions.

00:04:47.695 --> 00:04:53.240
[MUSIC]

00:04:53.240 --> 00:04:58.053
You think about Barack Obama, Ronald
Reagan, these are people who could intuit

00:04:58.053 --> 00:05:03.006
what a given audience needed, and say that
idea that a majority of people were going

00:05:03.006 --> 00:05:08.440
to turn out to be moved by and potentially
say different things to different groups.

00:05:08.440 --> 00:05:10.950
Now, we often think that
if you're saying different

00:05:10.950 --> 00:05:13.530
things to different groups that
that means you're a flip-flopper or

00:05:13.530 --> 00:05:17.030
you're insincere, or you're being
cynical or something like that.

00:05:17.030 --> 00:05:21.180
But I think that that's not really a
realistic critique in a polarized society.

00:05:21.180 --> 00:05:24.210
In a polarized society where
people value different things,

00:05:24.210 --> 00:05:26.542
they have incredibly diverse backgrounds.

00:05:26.542 --> 00:05:28.330
We're going to have to be
comfortable with the fact

00:05:28.330 --> 00:05:32.250
that people are going to have different
reasons for agreeing on things.

00:05:32.250 --> 00:05:36.700
That people are going to come to major
issues like inequality or climate change

00:05:36.700 --> 00:05:40.590
and potentially take the same side on
them, but for very different reasons.

00:05:40.590 --> 00:05:42.357
And if we're not comfortable with that,

00:05:42.357 --> 00:05:44.337
we're not going to get
political consensus.

00:05:44.337 --> 00:05:49.633
[MUSIC]

00:05:49.633 --> 00:05:53.593
I think that if we're going to resolve
all these tensions productively,

00:05:53.593 --> 00:05:57.157
it's going to mean that we somehow
turn towards one another and saw

00:05:57.157 --> 00:06:01.909
some humanity across political lines, like
maybe the existing strategies worked so

00:06:01.909 --> 00:06:04.483
badly, that we wound up
saying you know what,

00:06:04.483 --> 00:06:08.210
we're not going to do it that way,
the politics of division.

00:06:08.210 --> 00:06:10.720
They're not working,
they're hurting the country as a whole.

00:06:10.720 --> 00:06:14.990
And they're not really even helping my
side get a critical mass to pass anything.

00:06:14.990 --> 00:06:16.930
And so I'm going to try a different way.

00:06:16.930 --> 00:06:19.856
And then that's going to be us
turning towards each other and

00:06:19.856 --> 00:06:22.554
seeing a common humanity
that we struggle to see now.

00:06:22.554 --> 00:06:30.944
[MUSIC]

